WEBVTT

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.400
I have a favor to ask.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.400
Favor?

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.400
Could you arrange a meeting for me
with Chairman Maebara from the JSF?

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.350
With Chairman Maebara?

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.090
I'd like to talk to him.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.450
About what?

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.910
I can't say.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.180
No.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.390
Why not?

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:32.000
Chairman Maebara is a man who oversees the entirety of Japanese competitive swimming.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.290
He's not someone a mere high school diver like you can easily meet.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.180
I didn't come to hear excuses.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.090
There's something I have to talk to him about.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.030
I'm an Olympic representative.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.190
I believe I have the right to talk, at least.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.960
This is the first time I've ever come to you for anything.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.710
I'm asking you to help me just this once.

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:05.700
Please.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.800
Maybe this will be such a big decision.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:55.150
Anyway, it's an established fact that the much better diving environment is proved in U.S.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:57.150
What?

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.910
They look serious.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.120
I mentioned that I used to have an American tutor, right?

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.870
What about it?

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.650
He's asking her to come to the U.S.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:12.170
Huh?!

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.920
Coach Asaki is being recruited to overseas.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:16.950
No way.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:20.430
Okay.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:21.930
"Okay"?

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.010
Be quiet!

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.440
Do you think he was a scout?

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.180
Hey, that's it for the day!

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:58.710
Did you not hear me?!

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.900
Someone call an ambulance! Hurry!

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.130
You're still here?

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:35.620
How's Tomo?

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.970
He'll be getting a thorough checkup tomorrow.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.130
He'll be discharged if they don't find anything.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.170
I'm asking how he's holding up.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.200
Right.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.220
He's talking. He says he's sorry for putting everyone through this.

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:51.270
Let's go. I'll get the car.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:52.480
Thank you for everything.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.620
Oh, Youichi!

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:12.870
I think you've grown even more handsome since last I saw you.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.620
Anyway, come in. Everyone's waiting.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:17.190
Everyone?

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:21.750
You okay, Tomo?

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:23.760
Yeah, I mean...

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:25.260
What's with the flowers?

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.960
They're the orchids you gave me.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:28.900
What?

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.890
You can borrow them until you recover.

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:33.940
You didn't bring them from home, did you?

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.020
Yeah, I'm glad I've been training.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.390
Thanks, Youichi.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.800
Now that I've seen them again, those are some flowers!

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.530
I feel like a prince or something.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:48.030
You look well.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:54.040
Well, that pretty much confirms that I'm a jinx.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:56.250
I mean, doesn't it?

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.040
As soon as I get back from Tsugaru,
the Olympic representatives get picked,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.550
my rival starts skipping practice,
and he's been in a slump since returning.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:08.400
And now my other rival's been injured.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:09.800
Sorry.

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.560
A lot's been happening, huh?

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:20.360
Hey, Tomo, aren't you scared to dive now?

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:23.320
No, I'm probably okay.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:25.020
Okay.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.570
Now then, guess I should get going.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:31.300
What?

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.050
But you just got here.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:36.550
Are you stupid? We've got two rivals on the sidelines.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.320
When will we ever get a better chance?

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.080
Now run! Lift some weights!

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.410
Me? I've got this.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:45.730
Wow!

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.590
So you really <i>are</i> doing ballet.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.550
Of course I am!

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:51.840
Apparently, I'm pretty good at it too.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:53.570
Well, see you, Sakai.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:54.970
Come on, let's go.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:56.600
Thanks for coming, guys!

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:00.850
Hey, Tomo.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.710
I might be totally off base here, but...

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:04.860
What is it?

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.270
You seemed like you were rushing yourself.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.860
Did something happen before the accident?

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:11.300
No...

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.800
What is it? Tell me.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:25.170
I mean, I was just a little shocked.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:26.100
By what?

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.130
When the Olympic representatives were announced.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:35.490
Obviously, it was partly about not getting picked, but that wasn't all.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.200
I'm not sure how to say this.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:42.110
Do you remember when I said that I wanted to surpass the limits?

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:43.200
Yeah.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.750
I always felt trapped inside the limits the adults placed around me.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:52.160
And I thought I could surpass them through diving.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:53.160
Yeah.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:08:00.930
But, before I knew it, I started thinking that surpassing the limits meant making it to the Olympics.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:07.600
But when I listened to the reasons why you and Teramoto were selected,

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:14.600
I began to think that the Olympics were just
another one of these limits that the adults made.

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:20.840
Choosing representatives early to win medals, adjusting how many are selected—

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:26.050
it's like they're not our Olympics anymore or something.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:28.440
Our Olympics?

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:30.020
Yeah.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:36.590
When I started to think all there is at the center of the Olympics is just a bunch of weird adults I'd never met before,

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:39.450
everything just seemed stupid.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.320
That's why I wanted to do the four-and-a-half.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:47.460
I wanted to make the four-and-a-half my own limit.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.120
A limit I chose, and one for me to surpass.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:56.680
So I wouldn't let anyone get in my way.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:01.430
It's something where success would be apparent.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:05.230
I wanted a clear limit like that.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.790
That's why you rushed yourself and got hurt.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:13.680
I felt like I had plenty of time but sometimes not enough.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.240
All the years I've been diving just seemed to have flown by.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:21.240
Try jumping.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:25.170
I can't. I haven't even really been able to do it at a lower level.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.250
Stop giving up before you even try.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:32.760
It wasn't too long ago that you were just a scared little kid.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:42.680
One more time!

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:48.800
But once you decided to do something, you never gave up.

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:03.490
Until, one day, you finally did a
three-and-a-half right in front of me.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:13.830
I was happy.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:17.010
But to be honest, I was a little scared too.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.510
Scared because you were catching up to me before I even realized it.

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:23.630
I'm frustrated.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:28.770
I didn't know how bad it felt to lose a competition.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:30.580
I want to win!

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.890
I want to win next time and get first place!

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:35.900
You're serious, aren't you?

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:39.910
You're serious about pulling off the four-and-a-half.

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:50.420
I might not be able to do it now, but maybe I'll
grow wings or something in the next 12 months!

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:56.620
Why are you laughing?

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:59.340
Sorry, but that helped bring me to my senses.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.730
Our Olympics, huh?

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:05.180
You know, for me,

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:08.770
making it to the Olympics was always my dream.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:14.670
I was fine that I didn't have anything other than diving because I had that dream.

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:20.180
But even though I've always gotten what I wanted, I'm not happy at all.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:25.700
It's something I've always dreamed about,
something that's always been a part of me.

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:28.260
It's the reason I've worked so hard.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:31.960
And yet my heart just doesn't feel it.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.320
But now I've finally come to my senses.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:38.880
It's time to go back to square one.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:42.870
Anyway, you just focus on healing.

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:44.930
Youichi?

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:47.470
How stupid is it to send flowers to your enemy?

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.480
The prince only needs himself.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:54.670
Listen, Tomo.

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:55.980
I'll always be

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:58.290
number one.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:05.870
Thank you for your hospitality.

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:08.990
Wait, aren't those flowers...

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:26.180
Excuse me.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:30.060
Were you able to arrange a meeting with Chairman Maebara?

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.040
Yes, he'll meet with you.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:35.150
Really?

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:39.800
But if you don't tell me what you're going to talk about, I can't allow you to—

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:41.390
That, I can't tell you.

00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:42.260
Why?

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:43.820
Because I don't want to.

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.610
Even if I did tell you, you'd just take the chairman's side.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:51.430
You don't know that unless you tell me.

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:52.850
But I <i>do</i> know!

00:12:56.620 --> 00:12:59.090
It seems you've let this go to your head.

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:03.740
Yes, you're an Olympic representative.

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:06.180
You may have the right to meet with the chairman,

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:10.020
but if you were to somehow offend the chairman,

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:11.190
MDC won't be able to—

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:12.920
That's all you care about!

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:16.870
Even when I got hurt! Even when I was chosen!

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:20.310
You care more about MDC than me!

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:23.310
See? I knew it.

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.760
You haven't seen me at all.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:28.740
You don't care as long as you have the MDC! Right?!

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:31.560
I'm not happy at all.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:36.250
I got picked to go to the Olympics,
but absolutely nothing about it makes me happy!

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:41.560
These aren't your Olympics.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:43.260
They're mine!

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:46.380
Do whatever you want.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:02.520
What are you doing?

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:04.360
Um... well...

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:08.460
Is something troubling you?

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:12.350
I get that you're uneasy coming off an injury.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:13.760
That's not it.

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:15.240
Then what?

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:18.290
Well, it's really great, isn't it?

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:24.550
That MDC won't close because Youichi was chosen for the Olympics.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.300
Why are you bringing that up here?

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:30.680
Because that's the reason you came here, isn't it?

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:33.870
So I thought you'd be ready to move on.

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:35.870
And what if I was?

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:37.420
If you were, then...

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:42.820
it'd make me sad because it'll feel like you're leaving us behind.

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.070
Sorry, it's nothing.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:50.820
Pull yourself together.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:54.240
There's no "us" in diving.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:55.700
There's only you.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.560
Fight this battle alone.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:01.330
You're able to do that now.

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.390
<i>I couldn't say it.</i>

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:14.510
<i>I couldn't ask her why she was going to America.</i>

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:19.900
I came today because I have a favor to ask.

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:25.890
I...

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:30.150
I would like you to take my Olympic spot back.

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:37.140
Could you reset our standings and allow us to compete for the spots again in another competition?

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:44.120
That is a very strange idea, but also unrealistic.

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:47.320
Do you really think that's possible?

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:52.180
In any case, I will not be participating in the Japan-China meet at the end of the month.

00:15:54.130 --> 00:16:01.580
You're planning to use the results of that to announce
that Teramoto and I have been selected for the Olympics.

00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:06.310
But you couldn't possibly offer a spot to
someone who didn't compete in the meet.

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:12.350
So you'll take away your own spot.

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:16.040
I get it.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:18.380
You don't think it's fair, right?

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:21.960
But the Olympics aren't just yours.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:30.090
Just as you're trying to protect something, I have things I have to protect as well.

00:16:31.450 --> 00:16:33.590
Let me tell you, Mr. Fujitani...

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:38.600
I want a medal. I daresay, I want one much more than you do.

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:42.850
Even if I had to go to Hell to get it, I would do it in a heartbeat.

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:49.280
This is my greatest wish,
after spending decades here at the JSF.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:56.220
So I want to send the members I feel have the best
chance of making that happen to the next Olympics.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.240
That's why I chose you, someone with potential.

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:04.710
But if you're saying you'll withdraw from the Japan-China meet,

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:08.640
then I'll have to come up with a new plan.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:14.890
Like you said, I can't give the spot to someone who didn't participate in the meet.

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:20.360
If you really are intent on missing the meet,

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:24.140
then, unfortunately, your spot is revoked.

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:26.940
But in exchange,

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:36.520
I'll give your spot to any Japanese diver who scores more than 430 points at the Japan-China meet.

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:38.520
How does that sound to you?

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:46.440
Of course, there's a very good chance that Teramoto is the only diver capable of meeting this condition.

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:48.320
If that happens, then I'll have no choice.

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:53.730
We'll have to hold another competition to decide who earns the spot.

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:54.300
Competition?

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:57.800
I'm talking about an Olympic trial.

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:06.570
It'll be up to you to enter,
but the cutoff will still be 430 points.

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:13.650
The winner, and only the winner, will claim the
spot as long as he scores more than 430 points.

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:20.120
And if nobody is able to surpass this baseline, then I'll have no other choice.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:24.010
We'll have Teramoto go to the Olympics alone.

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:27.830
Listen to me.

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:32.540
Once you've thrown something away, getting it back is no easy matter.

00:18:32.540 --> 00:18:33.840
Understand?

00:18:35.310 --> 00:18:35.980
Yes, sir.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:41.850
<i>And so it was time for the Japan-China meet.</i>

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:41.850
Japan-China Meet

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:42.780
<i>I...</i>

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:53.710
<i>was unable to surpass my limit.</i>

00:18:55.200 --> 00:19:00.100
<i>In the end, nobody besides Teramoto was able to score above 430 points,</i>

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:04.320
<i>and the contest carried over to the Olympic trials.</i>

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:10.160
Congrats.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:18.260
After careful deliberation, Sakai Tomoki has been
declared the undisputed world champion of stupidity.

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:20.390
Thanks.

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:22.760
Is that something prestigious?

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:25.310
Obviously not, idiot!

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:29.520
You would've gotten 430 points if you played it safe and went with the three-and-a-half.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:35.100
What kind of idiot throws away their
chance at a spot on the Olympic team?

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:40.530
If you're talking about an idiot who just hands
back a spot he was given, there's one right here.

00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:43.370
Shut up! That's better than Tomo, at least!

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:48.080
Even if you managed to pull off the four-and-a-half,

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:52.540
you would've gotten zero points for doing a dive you never submitted to the judges.

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:56.550
You won't find anyone else in the world this stupid.

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:00.800
Honestly. We're all idiots.

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:07.900
But you knew all that already and were set to do it anyway, right?

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:13.320
That's because you brought the Olympics back for us.

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:18.570
That's why I wanted to take it back too.

00:20:18.860 --> 00:20:20.320
On my own.

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:25.070
I may've failed the dive, but I did get it back.

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:30.950
I felt like I finally made a breakthrough with the four-and-a-half today.

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:35.510
It's the first time I've actually felt like I could pull it off.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:39.960
I'll definitely pull it off at the trials in December.

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:45.230
I'll do more revolutions than anyone else,
score those 430 points,

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:48.290
and make it to the Olympics!

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:54.730
You're not going to the Olympics.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:20:59.050
I'll stop you.

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:02.040
No.

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:05.990
We are!
